message: "If you use this research code, please cite this software archive and the relevant thesis or chapter publication."
type: software
title: "Dispersal and Connectivity in Physical, Seasonal, and Extreme Landscapes: PhD Thesis"
version: "1.0.1"
authors:
  - family-names: "Hofmann"
    given-names: "David D."
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3477-4365"
    affiliation: "University of Zurich"
repository-code: "https://github.com/DavidDHofmann/PhD"
url: "https://github.com/DavidDHofmann/PhD"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
abstract: >-
  Research code accompanying David D. Hofmann's PhD thesis, Dispersal and
  Connectivity in Physical, Seasonal, and Extreme Landscapes, at the
  University of Zurich (https://doi.org/10.5167/uzh-263215). The repository
  contains workflows for data preparation, movement modelling,
  individual-based dispersal simulations, connectivity calculations, and
  visualization across four thesis chapters, primarily in R with supporting
  C++ functions. It also contains manuscript and thesis sources, figures,
  and defense material. Input datasets are distributed separately through
  the repositories and access arrangements described in the documentation.
keywords:
  - "movement ecology"
  - "landscape connectivity"
  - "animal dispersal"
  - "integrated step-selection functions"
  - "individual-based simulation"
  - "seasonality"
  - "climate change"
  - "African wild dog"
  - "Lycaon pictus"
  - "R"
